-- title:
--   One point over supersingular nodes with j=0 or 1728
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $q \ge 5$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$, and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$. Let $a \in k$ satisfy: $a$ lies in `ssJSet q k`, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $k$ with $j$-invariant $a$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$; $a^{q^2} = a$; and $a = 0$ or $a = 1728$. Let $f$ lie in `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full level-$q$ modular function field inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}((t))$. Assume $f$, viewed as a Laurent series, is integral over `NodeLocalized.modularLocalizedAtPoint (1 * q) A.toSubring red a (a ^ q)`, the ring of Laurent series $g$ admitting $r, s \in \mathrm{MvPolynomial}(\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\ A$ with `pointEval A red a (a ^ q) s ≠ 0` and $g \cdot \mathrm{modularEval}(s) = \mathrm{modularEval}(r)$. Assume further that $f$ and its image under `frickeInvolutionBar (1 * q)` both lie in `CharPReduction.modularLocalized (1 * q) A.toSubring red`, the localisation of the subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((t))$ generated by the constant series from $A$ together with $j(q_\tau)$ and $j(q_\tau^{q})$ at the complement of the kernel of reduction, and that the images of both under `CharPReduction.modularRedLocHom` lie in `modularFunctionFieldC k 1`, the subfield of $k((t))$ generated over $k$ by `jqModC k` and `jqNModC k 1`. The conclusion is that there exists $c \in k$ such that both components of `frobNodePair q a`, namely the places `charLGeomPlaceOfPoint k a` and `charLGeomPlaceOfPoint k (a ^ q)`, take the value $c$ (in the sense that the relevant function lies in the valuation subring of the place and its residue is the image of $c$) at the reduction of $f$, respectively at the reduction of the Fricke transform of $f$.
--
--   This is the statement that the two branches of $X_0(q)$ through a supersingular node with $j$-invariant $0$ or $1728$ still carry a single point, so that a function integral over the local ring of the node assumes equal values on the two branches; in the classical theory this is the width $2$ or $3$ phenomenon at the elliptic points of the Deligne–Rapoport model. It feeds the degenerate case of the branch-value comparison, [`ModularCurve.exists_hasValue_frobNodePair_of_isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_of_degenerate`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_hasValue_frobNodePair_of_isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_of_degenerate).
